    },
    {
      "id": "KxRJgC2leOJ5IXIXgEXwe",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -226.77982261032793,
      "y": 1861.1922723831951,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 1040,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#52575c",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2X",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 43231870,
      "version": 590,
      "versionNonce": 1539727678,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1793918278398,
      "created": 1793915790515,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Client generates file chunks and SHA-256 hashes\n    - The client splits the large file into smaller chunks.\n    - It calculates the SHA-256 hash for each chunk before uploading.\n    - It maintains the chunk number, chunk size, and corresponding SHA-256 hash.\n2. Client initiates the upload with the backend\n    - The client sends an InitiateUpload request to file-uploader-srvc.\n    - The request contains:\n        - File name and file size.\n        - Total number of chunks.\n        - Chunk numbers and sizes.\n        - Expected SHA-256 hash of each chunk.\n    - The backend generates a unique fileId and S3 object key.\n    - The backend initiates an S3 multipart upload using CreateMultipartUpload.\n    - S3 returns a unique uploadId.\n    - The backend stores the upload session and expected chunk metadata in Redis/database.\n    - The backend generates pre-signed UploadPart URLs for the individual chunks and returns \n       them to the client.\n3. Client uploads chunks directly to S3\n    - The client uploads each chunk directly to S3 using its corresponding pre-signed URL\n    - Each request includes:\n          - Multipart uploadId, Part number, Chunk data, SHA-256 checksum (x-amz-checksum-sha256).\n    - S3 validates the checksum against the uploaded chunk data.\n    - After successful upload, S3 returns: ETag, Checksum SHA-256 (if configured).\n    - The client collects the ETag and checksum for every successfully uploaded part.\n    - The backend server never handles the actual file data.\n4. Client notifies backend about uploaded chunks\n    -After all chunks are uploaded, the client sends a CompleteUpload request to file-uploader-srvc.\n    -The request contains:\n         - fileId, uploadId, Part numbers, ETags, SHA-256 checksums.\n    - The backend retrieves the expected chunk metadata from Redis/database.\n    - The backend calls S3 ListParts API to retrieve the actual uploaded part information.\n    - It verifies:\n         - All expected parts are uploaded.\n         - No parts are missing.\n         - Part numbers and sizes match.\n         - Actual SHA-256 checksums match the expected hashes.\n         - ETags match the uploaded parts.\n5. Backend completes the S3 multipart upload\n    - Once all parts pass validation, the backend calls S3 CompleteMultipartUpload.\n    - It provides the ordered list of part numbers and ETags.\n    - S3 assembles all parts into the final object.\n    - S3 returns a successful completion response.\n    - The backend updates the status to COMPLETION_PENDING.\n    - S3 publishes an ObjectCreated:CompleteMultipartUpload event through the \n       configured SQS/EventBridge integration.\n6. Backend confirms the complete file upload\n    - file-uploader-srvc consumes the S3 completion event.\n    - It identifies the file using the S3 bucket and object key.\n    - The backend calls S3 HeadObject to verify the final object and its configured checksum metadata.\n    - It confirms the final object exists and the upload is consistent.\n    - The backend updates Redis/database status to UPLOAD_COMPLETED.\n    - The client retrieves the final upload status through the backend API.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Client generates file chunks and SHA-256 hashes\n    - The client splits the large file into smaller chunks.\n    - It calculates the SHA-256 hash for each chunk before uploading.\n    - It maintains the chunk number, chunk size, and corresponding SHA-256 hash.\n2. Client initiates the upload with the backend\n    - The client sends an InitiateUpload request to file-uploader-srvc.\n    - The request contains:\n        - File name and file size.\n        - Total number of chunks.\n        - Chunk numbers and sizes.\n        - Expected SHA-256 hash of each chunk.\n    - The backend generates a unique fileId and S3 object key.\n    - The backend initiates an S3 multipart upload using CreateMultipartUpload.\n    - S3 returns a unique uploadId.\n    - The backend stores the upload session and expected chunk metadata in Redis/database.\n    - The backend generates pre-signed UploadPart URLs for the individual chunks and returns \n       them to the client.\n3. Client uploads chunks directly to S3\n    - The client uploads each chunk directly to S3 using its corresponding pre-signed URL\n    - Each request includes:\n          - Multipart uploadId, Part number, Chunk data, SHA-256 checksum (x-amz-checksum-sha256).\n    - S3 validates the checksum against the uploaded chunk data.\n    - After successful upload, S3 returns: ETag, Checksum SHA-256 (if configured).\n    - The client collects the ETag and checksum for every successfully uploaded part.\n    - The backend server never handles the actual file data.\n4. Client notifies backend about uploaded chunks\n    -After all chunks are uploaded, the client sends a CompleteUpload request to file-uploader-srvc.\n    -The request contains:\n         - fileId, uploadId, Part numbers, ETags, SHA-256 checksums.\n    - The backend retrieves the expected chunk metadata from Redis/database.\n    - The backend calls S3 ListParts API to retrieve the actual uploaded part information.\n    - It verifies:\n         - All expected parts are uploaded.\n         - No parts are missing.\n         - Part numbers and sizes match.\n         - Actual SHA-256 checksums match the expected hashes.\n         - ETags match the uploaded parts.\n5. Backend completes the S3 multipart upload\n    - Once all parts pass validation, the backend calls S3 CompleteMultipartUpload.\n    - It provides the ordered list of part numbers and ETags.\n    - S3 assembles all parts into the final object.\n    - S3 returns a successful completion response.\n    - The backend updates the status to COMPLETION_PENDING.\n    - S3 publishes an ObjectCreated:CompleteMultipartUpload event through the \n       configured SQS/EventBridge integration.\n6. Backend confirms the complete file upload\n    - file-uploader-srvc consumes the S3 completion event.\n    - It identifies the file using the S3 bucket and object key.\n    - The backend calls S3 HeadObject to verify the final object and its configured checksum metadata.\n    - It confirms the final object exists and the upload is consistent.\n    - The backend updates Redis/database status to UPLOAD_COMPLETED.\n    - The client retrieves the final upload status through the backend API.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
